Fixed the token endpoint refusing most OAuth clients. It read `client_id` and `client_secret` from the form only, while RFC 6749 §2.3.1 requires a server to accept HTTP Basic and makes the form the optional half. authlib's default is `client_secret_basic`, so this package's own relying party was refused by this package's own authorization server with `invalid_client` — a failure that reads as a wrong secret and is not one. `oauth2-proxy` and most off-the-shelf clients default the same way.

The endpoint now accepts both, and discovery advertises `client_secret_basic` alongside the form. Credentials sent by both means at once are refused rather than reconciled, as §2.3 requires, and a `client_id` in the form that disagrees with the one in the header is refused rather than resolved in either direction. A Basic header that cannot be decoded refuses the request instead of falling through to the form, which would authenticate a request whose header could not be read. The header is taken off `request._auth`, because ZPublisher moves it there and out of the environment during request construction.

Found by the federation stack, standing two of these sites up and pointing one at the other. Neither end was wrong on its own; they simply could not agree. @ericof
